WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.546
These readings ask who pauses whom when a technology moves fast.

00:00:04.546 --> 00:00:09.376
We look at scientists restraining their own work on recombinant DNA,

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:14.916
a government trying to shape encryption, and a recent call to pause artificial

00:00:14.916 --> 00:00:15.840
intelligence.

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:21.304
Jasanoff, Saha and Hurlbut argue that Asilomar narrowed the debate to risks

00:00:21.304 --> 00:00:22.834
scientists knew best.

00:00:22.834 --> 00:00:26.477
Containment inside the lab became the main answer.

00:00:26.477 --> 00:00:32.160
Research stayed nearly free from oversight, and the public was asked to defer.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.750
The authors say expertise and social responsibility were present at Asilomar,

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:39.347
but diversity was not.

00:00:39.347 --> 00:00:43.485
Questions that were left out, like ecology and ownership,

00:00:43.485 --> 00:00:48.640
later returned as bitter public fights over genetically modified crops.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.046
In 1993 the White House announced the Clipper Chip.

00:00:52.046 --> 00:00:57.389
It presented the chip as a way to protect privacy while meeting the needs of law

00:00:57.389 --> 00:00:58.191
enforcement.

00:00:58.191 --> 00:01:02.800
This was a government-led approach rather than a pause by scientists.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.599
Under key escrow, each device's key is split in two.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:11.421
The halves are stored separately, run by two independent entities.

00:01:11.421 --> 00:01:17.120
Officials could get them only after showing legal authorization for a wiretap.

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:22.211
The statement claimed the system was strong and offered an independent panel of

00:01:22.211 --> 00:01:24.210
experts to check the algorithm.

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:28.141
It argued that privacy and law enforcement could be balanced,

00:01:28.141 --> 00:01:30.720
and that the supposed tension was false.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:36.666
The Future of Life Institute letter asked all AI labs to pause training of systems

00:01:36.666 --> 00:01:39.566
more powerful than GPT-4 for six months.

00:01:39.566 --> 00:01:45.440
If labs would not, it said governments should step in and institute a moratorium.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:49.661
The common question is where the choice was, and who made it.

00:01:49.661 --> 00:01:54.989
The AI letter says such decisions must not be left to unelected tech leaders.

00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:59.280
Ask whether Asilomar and Clipper really support that argument.
